WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.940
Film Courage: How did you get your first job
as a director/filmmaker?

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:07.080
Dale Stelly, Filmmaker: How did I get my first
job?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:16.260
My first music video that I shot on Super
16 millimeter was for an up-and-coming local

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:22.980
hip-hop artist named Power Man and to make
long story short I met him at Roscoe’s Chicken

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:29.580
and Waffles and me working on a lot of music
videos I was ready to start directing.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.020
This was like 1996?

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.621
No, I didn’t start my company then.

00:00:36.621 --> 00:00:40.070
I started directing and I didn’t start my
company until 1998.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:47.140
But from ’96 to ’98 I started getting
the confidence to start my company and everything.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:55.571
The first video that I directed on film was
for an artist named Power Man and it was a

00:00:55.571 --> 00:01:03.960
12,000 budget which at that time for me was
a decent budget and I shot a great video with

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.170
some great post-production hookups that I
had when I was working at Cinesite so I was

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:15.870
able to incorporate some green screen and
take my directing debut to the best level

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.580
that I can with the resources and the people
that I knew that kind of helped me pull that

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.600
video off and I was on my way from that video.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.410
I started directing one music video after
the next.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:34.159
Being able to shoot on film and it looked
professional.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:38.920
At that time it was very hard to get your
hands on film unless you had a real budget.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.060
You had to rent the film camera and you need
someone who needs to know how shoot film and

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:50.780
change the film mags and a whole different
filmmaking process so I really feel good about

00:01:50.780 --> 00:02:00.710
my journey because I came from actually filmmaking
background originating my first films on 35

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.820
mm is way different from shooting on digital
cameras now.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.051
If you’re shooting on film you’ve got
to get the film developed and then you’ve

00:02:09.051 --> 00:02:16.020
got to go through telecine and it takes way
more skill to me to shoot something on 35

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.360
millimeter in and then to raise up an HD camera
and press the auto settings you know what

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.360
I mean?

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.260
And think that you’re making a movie or
whatever.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.240
Which is pretty much what’s going on today.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:33.840
These digital cameras are making everything
as easy as possible to acquire image and it

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:41.390
just kind of takes to me the filmmaking fun
out it when you don’t need a light meter

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.060
any more.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:47.569
Back in the day shooting film you would measure
the light, measure the distance from the lens

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:48.630
to the actor.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.790
It’s just different.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.940
Still at the end of the day all people care
about is the end product but as a filmmaker

00:02:54.940 --> 00:03:01.720
I’m just glad that I came from actual filmmaking
days in transition into the digital world

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:09.930
which makes me feel like an asset to a lot
of different productions when I come to the

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:15.790
table, to be intelligent across the whole
board when it comes to making a movie on film

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:16.790
or movie on digital.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:23.200
Because some directors still…like Tarantino
I think he’s still shooting 35 millimeter.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:30.460
People still have a love and respect for film
but it’s still a dying situation that some

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.120
of us are trying to keep alive.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.580
Film Courage: Do you remember your call time
on that music video?

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.140
What was that morning like?

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:44.129
Dale: 8:00 a.m. and I remember shooting most
of the video in my neighborhood because I’ve

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:49.470
lived in my neighborhood for ever and ever
and everyone knew me and all the different

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:56.191
small businesses and my parents had a nice
house on the corner and I knew a bunch of

00:03:56.191 --> 00:03:57.191
people with cars and stuff.

00:03:57.191 --> 00:04:03.950
So I pulled off a cool little hip-hop video
in the neighborhood that came out nice for

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:04.950
a rap artist.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:10.440
And I think we shot like 12 hours (8:00 am
to 8:00 pm) or something like that which was

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:17.120
standard filmmaking hours (12 hour days) and
it was exciting to shoot my first video on

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:25.219
film, on an ARRI SR3 camera, 24 frames per
second, all that good stuff when you are on

00:04:25.219 --> 00:04:26.850
set you’re like “Wow?

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.669
I’m shooting my first actual film.”

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:30.900
It was dope.

